WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.677
CASE and FILTER can express similar conditional aggregation logic.

00:00:04.677 --> 00:00:12.177
CASE is generally more portable because many SQL systems support conditional expressions inside aggregates.

00:00:12.177 --> 00:00:19.262
FILTER is concise and readable in PostgreSQL, especially when several measures each have their own condition.

00:00:19.262 --> 00:00:22.512
For this course, you should understand both.

00:00:22.512 --> 00:00:27.572
If you know the query will run in PostgreSQL, FILTER can be a clean choice.

00:00:27.572 --> 00:00:33.812
If you expect the SQL to move between different platforms, SUM with CASE is often safer.

00:00:33.812 --> 00:00:39.137
Whichever style you choose, validate the measure using a small known example.

00:00:39.137 --> 00:00:43.492
Conditional metrics can look correct while using slightly different conditions,

00:00:43.492 --> 00:00:47.192
so checking a few rows manually is still important.

00:00:47.192 --> 00:00:54.382
Conditional aggregation lets one grouped query calculate several targeted measures.

00:00:54.382 --> 00:00:58.747
For example, a department row can show total admissions, emergency admissions,

00:00:58.747 --> 00:01:01.437
and elective admissions side by side.

00:01:01.437 --> 00:01:06.362
FILTER is concise in PostgreSQL; CASE is widely portable.

00:01:06.362 --> 00:01:09.581
Both require you to define the condition precisely.
